course: books
lesson: 19
title: Alice in Wonderland, chapter X, part 1: The Lobster Quadrille (a dance, a whiting and a porpoise)
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The Mock Turtle sobs so hard he can't speak, and the Gryphon thumps him on the back as if he had a bone stuck in his throat. Then the two creatures excitedly explain the Lobster Quadrille, a seaside dance: two lines of seals, turtles and salmon, each with a lobster for a partner — and then you throw the lobsters as far out to sea as you can!
explain: They jump about like mad things while explaining, then suddenly sit down sadly and quietly. To show Alice, they dance round and round her, treading on her toes, while the Mock Turtle sings, slowly and sadly, «Will you walk a little faster?» said a whiting to a snail.
explain: Alice is very careful now. Twice she almost says she has eaten these creatures — «I once tasted—» lobster, «I've often seen them at dinn—» — and stops herself just in time. The Mock Turtle thinks «Dinn» must be a place.
explain: The Gryphon explains, very seriously, why whitings have their tails in their mouths: they were thrown out to sea with the lobsters, fell a long way, and their tails got stuck. Then come more puns: whiting is what «does the boots and shoes» under the sea, and shoes there are made of «soles and eels».
explain: Last joke: no wise fish goes on a journey «without a porpoise». «Don't you mean purpose?» asks Alice. «I mean what I say,» answers the Mock Turtle, offended — the very words that tangled Alice up at the tea-party.
fact: The Gryphon thumps the sobbing Mock Turtle: «Same as if he had a bone in his throat.»
fact: Alice begins «I once tasted—» but checks herself and says «No, never» — she won't tell a turtle she has eaten lobster.
fact: A quadrille is an old-fashioned dance for couples, done in steps called «figures»; the Lobster Quadrille is Carroll's made-up sea version.
fact: The dance: two lines on the sea-shore (seals, turtles, salmon and so on), clear the jelly-fish out of the way, advance twice, each with a lobster as partner, set to partners, change lobsters, retire.
fact: Then «you throw the— The lobsters!— as far out to sea as you can», swim after them, turn a somersault, change lobsters again, and come back to land: «that's all the first figure»; to show Alice they dance it with no lobsters at all: «We can do without lobsters, you know.»
fact: The song: «"Will you walk a little faster?" said a whiting to a snail. "There's a porpoise close behind us, and he's treading on my tail."»
fact: The snail says «Too far, too far!» and will not join the dance; the whiting answers «The further off from England the nearer is to France»; when the song ends, Alice is «very glad that it was over at last».
fact: «Shingle» is a beach of small round pebbles; «askance» means sideways, with doubt; «a whiting» is a small sea fish.
fact: Alice starts «I've often seen them at dinn—» (dinner) and stops; the Mock Turtle says «I don't know where Dinn may be».
fact: Cooks in Alice's time served whiting curled with the tail in its mouth and covered in breadcrumbs; the Mock Turtle says the crumbs «would all wash off in the sea».
fact: The Gryphon's reason: they went with the lobsters to the dance, got thrown out to sea, fell a long way, got their tails fast in their mouths, and couldn't get them out again.
fact: «It does the boots and shoes»: whiting is also an old word for a white chalk powder used for cleaning; shoes on land are «done with blacking», black polish.
fact: Under the sea, shoes are made of «soles and eels» — a pun on soles and heels; a sole is also a flat fish.
fact: «No wise fish would go anywhere without a porpoise» — porpoise (a sea animal like a small dolphin) sounds like purpose (a reason); «I mean what I say,» says the Mock Turtle; Alice, still thinking of the song, says that if she had been the whiting she would have told the porpoise «Keep back, please: we don't want you with us!»
fact: Talk in this lesson: «heaps» (Australia, NZ) = lots, very; «togs» (Australia) = swimming costume; «craic» (Ireland) = fun, a good time; «mid» = average, nothing special; «I'm dead» = that's so funny.
exercise: Why does the Gryphon shake and punch the Mock Turtle in the back? | Sobs choked his voice, «same as if he had a bone in his throat» | He had fallen asleep in the middle of his sad story and was snoring very loudly on the rock | He was rude | He was cold
exercise: Alice begins «I once tasted—». What was she about to say? | That she had once tasted lobster | That she had once tasted turtle soup made by the Duchess's cook | That she tasted sea water | That she tasted treacle
exercise: Why does Alice stop herself and say «No, never»? | It would be unkind to tell sea creatures she had eaten one | She really had never seen or tasted a single lobster anywhere in her whole life | The Gryphon told her to stop | She forgot the word
exercise: What is a quadrille? | An old-fashioned dance for couples | A kind of lobster that lives in pairs | A song | A school lesson in the sea
exercise: Who forms the two lines of the Lobster Quadrille? | Seals, turtles, salmon and so on | Cards, soldiers and courtiers in pairs, holding hands | Hedgehogs and flamingoes | Alice and the Queen
exercise: What must you clear out of the way first? | All the jelly-fish | All the big grey rocks | All the sharks and the whales | All the slow snails
exercise: Who is each dancer's partner? | A lobster | A small whiting | A slow snail | A grey seal
exercise: What do you do with the lobsters in the middle of the dance? | Throw them as far out to sea as you can | Cook them | Give them back to the Queen of Hearts on a velvet cushion | Teach them to sing
exercise: How do the Mock Turtle and the Gryphon behave while explaining the dance? | Jumping about like mad things, then suddenly sitting sadly and quietly | Calm and slow the whole time, speaking in low, serious voices like teachers | Asleep | Angry with Alice
exercise: When they dance it for Alice, what goes wrong? | They tread on her toes when they pass too close | They throw Alice out to sea instead of a lobster | They fall in the water | They forget the song
exercise: Why do they dance without lobsters? | The Mock Turtle says «We can do without lobsters, you know» | All the lobsters had been eaten at the Queen's dinner the night before | The Queen took them | Alice is afraid of lobsters
exercise: In the song, who asks «Will you walk a little faster?» and to whom? | A whiting, to a snail | A slow snail, to a whiting | A lobster, to a turtle | A porpoise, to a young seal
exercise: Who is «close behind us» and «treading on my tail»? | A porpoise | A lobster | A turtle | A shark
exercise: What does the snail answer about the dance? | «Too far, too far!» — he will not join | «Yes, at once, with all my heart, and I'll bring my friends!» | «Only if I may lead» | «I can't swim»
exercise: «The further off from England the nearer is to France.» Who says this in the song? | The whiting, his «scaly friend» | The snail, who wants to stay at home | The lobster | The Gryphon
exercise: How does Alice feel when the dance is over? | Very glad that it was over at last | Sad, because she wanted it to go on for hours and hours | Angry | Sleepy
exercise: «I've often seen them at dinn—» What does the Mock Turtle think «Dinn» is? | A place | A kind of fish | A kind of dance | A real person
exercise: What does Alice say whitings look like? | They have their tails in their mouths, and they're all over crumbs | They are long and green, with a curly shell on the back like a snail | They are pink | They have no tails
exercise: Why, says the Mock Turtle, is Alice wrong about the crumbs? | Crumbs would all wash off in the sea | Whitings eat all their crumbs before dinner | There's no bread under the sea | Crumbs are only for birds
exercise: How did whitings get their tails in their mouths, according to the Gryphon? | They were thrown out to sea, fell a long way, and their tails got stuck | They were born that way, like all fish, and never knew any different in their lives | They were biting their own tails for fun | A cook put them there
exercise: «Why it's called a whiting?» What does the Gryphon say it does? | «It does the boots and shoes» | «It whitens the whole sea every morning» | «It writes letters to the Queen» | «It does all the washing on Monday»
exercise: What are Alice's shoes «done with», to make them shiny? | Blacking | Whiting | Treacle | Butter
exercise: Under the sea, what are boots and shoes made of, says the Gryphon? | Soles and eels | Shells and pearls | Seaweed and sand | Old lobster shells
exercise: «If I'd been the whiting» — what would Alice have told the porpoise? | «Keep back, please: we don't want you with us!» | «Come and dance with us, the more the merrier!» | «Swim faster» | «Go and find the snail»
exercise: «With what porpoise?» What word does Alice think he means? | Purpose | Porridge | A promise | Porpoise is right
test: Ella: «Alice told them she'd scoffed loads of lobster, I'm dead.» (scoffed = eaten greedily; I'm dead = so funny) Put it right. | She began «I once tasted—» but stopped and said «No, never» | Right: she told them she ate lobster every Sunday at home with her sister and enjoyed it heaps | She said lobsters were mid | She asked for some
test: What would happen to lobsters in a real Lobster Quadrille, if it were done as the creatures describe? | They'd be flung out to sea in the middle of the dance | They would lead the dance and sing the song | They'd be cooked for supper by the Duchess's cook straight after the first figure | They'd be put on the throne
test: Ruby from Sydney: «they wore their togs and danced on the sand, sweet as.» (togs = swimming costume; sweet as = great) What does the book say about the dancing? | They danced round and round Alice, treading on her toes, singing slowly and sadly | They danced on the shingle in swimming costumes, quickly and happily | They didn't dance at all | They danced with real lobsters
test: «Then turn not pale, beloved snail, but come and join the dance.» What is the whiting trying to do? | Persuade the scared snail that being thrown far out to sea is fine | Tell the snail to go home before the sea gets too rough to swim in | Make fun of France | Win a race
test: Why does Alice check herself at «dinn—»? | She was about to say she had seen whitings at dinner, on a plate | She forgot the word «dinner» for a moment, because her head was so full of the dance | She was out of breath | The Gryphon pinched her
test: «They have their tails in their mouths» — why did Alice think this? | Whitings were served curled up like that, with crumbs, in her time | She had seen them swimming that way in the sea when she went to the seaside once | The Duchess told her | It's in the song
test: Is this TRUE or FALSE? «The Gryphon's explanation of the whiting's tail is a real fact about fish.» | False: it's a made-up chain of «so... so... so...» to sound reasonable | True: scientists found whitings bite their tails after falling into the sea | True: that is how cooks catch them | False: whitings have no tails
test: Max, chuffed: «the Gryphon said shoes under the sea are made of soles and heels.» What did he really say, and what's the joke? | «Soles and eels»: «eels» sounds like «heels», and a sole is a fish | «Soles and heels», with no joke at all | «Shells and pearls» | «Seaweed and leather»
test: «Whiting» does the boots and shoes. Which two meanings of «whiting» make the joke? | A small fish, and an old white cleaning powder | A white flower in the garden, and a white cat called Dinah | A tooth-brush and a scrubbing brush | The colour white and a kind of ink
test: «I mean what I say,» says the Mock Turtle, offended. What is funny about this? | He means «porpoise», and «porpoise» makes no sense — yet he's sure he means it | He is saying «purpose» very clearly, but Alice cannot hear him over the waves | He is speaking French | He is telling the truth about porpoises
test: Looking back: at the tea-party, who told Alice «Then you should say what you mean»? | The March Hare | The Hatter himself | The sleepy Dormouse | The Cheshire Cat
test: Looking back: in chapter II, Alice upset the Mouse again and again. What did she keep talking about? | Her cat Dinah, «such a capital one for catching mice», and a dog that kills rats | Lobsters and turtles, and how nice they both taste in soup with a bit of pepper | The Queen of Hearts | The tea-party
test: Looking back: in chapter III, the birds all hurried off. What had Alice said? | That Dinah would «eat a little bird as soon as look at it» | That the Dodo was a silly old bird who couldn't even run a race | That the race was unfair | That she was very tired
test: Looking back: how has Alice changed, from the Mouse in chapter II to the Mock Turtle in chapter X? | She now stops herself before saying something that would upset the creatures | She now talks about Dinah even more, to everyone she meets | She has stopped talking at all | She is ruder
test: Looking back: in chapter II, why did Alice think the Mouse might be French? | She thought it might have «come over with William the Conqueror» | It spoke French to her | It had a French hat | The Duchess told her
test: Looking back: in chapter II, what did Alice believe you always find at the English seaside? | Bathing machines, children digging, lodging houses and a railway station | Lobsters dancing a quadrille | Treacle-wells | Flamingoes
test: ★★★ Count the steps the creatures shout out: advance twice, set to partners, change lobsters, retire, throw the lobsters, swim after them, somersault, change lobsters again, back to land. How many times do you «change lobsters»? | 2 | 1 | 3 | 4
test: ★★★ «Porpoise» and «purpose», «soles» and «souls», «whiting» and «whitening». Which of these pairs is NOT used as a joke in this part? | Soles and souls | Porpoise and purpose | Soles and the fish called sole | Whiting the fish and whiting the polish
test: ★★★ «The further off from England the nearer is to France.» When is this true? | When you are crossing the sea between England and France | Always, anywhere on earth | Never, because France is far away | Only for snails
exam: Identify the action which the Mock Turtle and the Gryphon describe as constituting a principal step of the Lobster Quadrille. | The throwing of the lobsters as far out to sea as possible | The consumption of the lobsters by the seals and turtles upon the shore at the conclusion of the figure | The painting of the lobsters red | The singing of the lobsters
exam: It is given that Alice twice commenced a remark concerning the eating of sea creatures and desisted. Select the response which most accurately characterises her reason. | A wish not to give offence to her companions | A failure of memory | A command from the Queen | An attack of sneezing
exam: Ascertain the cause assigned by the Gryphon for the whiting's tail being found in its mouth. | Its having been thrown out to sea with the lobsters and having fallen a long way | Its preparation by a cook | A habit acquired at school | A punishment ordered by the Queen
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the snail in the Mock Turtle's song? | He declined to join the dance, judging the distance too great | He joined the dance with enthusiasm and was thrown furthest of all the dancers | He was the porpoise's partner | He was thrown out to sea
